WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.888
Technical OSINT uses lawfully accessible public indicators to help defenders understand internet-facing exposure.

00:00:05.888 --> 00:00:09.895
Domains, DNS, addresses, hosting, certificates, web metadata, repositories, packages,

00:00:09.895 --> 00:00:14.792
and cloud references provide evidence, but rarely provide complete answers alone.

00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:18.354
Interpret naming and DNS in time and context.

00:00:18.354 --> 00:00:23.251
Distinguish address-range ownership from application control, and account for cloud platforms,

00:00:23.251 --> 00:00:26.813
shared hosts, content-delivery networks, proxies, and geolocation uncertainty.

00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:32.156
Certificate and web records can reveal names and technology clues without proving

00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:34.382
current security or permanent ownership.

00:00:34.382 --> 00:00:38.389
Public code and package artifacts can inform dependency review,

00:00:38.389 --> 00:00:41.505
but accidental secrets must never be used.

00:00:41.505 --> 00:00:46.848
Correlation works when every relationship has a source, date, meaning, and confidence.

00:00:46.848 --> 00:00:50.410
Test alternatives, preserve timelines, and avoid false attribution.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:54.417
Report unknown assets, stale records, development exposure, impersonation indicators,

00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:58.423
and third-party dependencies as risk signals requiring authorized validation.

00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:03.321
The objective is responsible visibility and decision support, not intrusive testing.

00:01:03.321 --> 00:01:06.883
Strong technical OSINT is scoped, documented, cautious, ethical,

00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:11.780
and connected to internal owners who can confirm and remediate findings.
